msgid "Rebuild your container images"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Your existing container images still contain the vulnerable JDK.\n"
"Fixed base images do not change images you already built, nor containers already running.\n"
"You have to *rebuild* your container images on top of the fixed base image, then *redeploy* them."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Docker and Podman reuse the base image cached locally.\n"
"Make sure the build uses the fixed one:"
msgstr ""

msgid "With a `Dockerfile`: build with `docker build --pull` (or `podman build --pull=newer`), or run `docker pull <base image>` before building, including when the build is run by the Quarkus Docker or Podman extensions."
msgstr ""

msgid "With Jib: the base image tag is resolved against the registry at each build, so the next build uses the fixed image."
msgstr ""

msgid "If you pinned the base image to a build tag (e.g. `1.24-3`) or a digest (`@sha256:...`), update it to the fixed build listed above."
msgstr ""

msgid "To check that an image ships a fixed JDK:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The JDK version must be at least 17.0.21.1, 21.0.13.1 or 25.0.5.1.\n"
"Vulnerable images show 17.0.21, 21.0.13 or 25.0.5 (without the trailing `.1`), or older."
msgstr ""

msgid "Quarkus 3.27: switch to the <code>1.24</code> images"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Quarkus 3.27 uses the UBI 9 `1.23` images by default (`registry.access.redhat.com/ubi9/openjdk-<version>-runtime:1.23`, in the generated `Dockerfile.jvm` and as the default base image of the container image extensions).\n"
"This stream is no longer maintained: it was *not* fixed and will not be."
msgstr ""

msgid "If you are using Quarkus 3.27, switch to the `1.24` images:"
msgstr ""

msgid "In your `Dockerfile.jvm`: `FROM registry.access.redhat.com/ubi9/openjdk-21-runtime:1.24` (or `openjdk-17-runtime:1.24`)."
msgstr ""

msgid "With Jib: `quarkus.jib.base-jvm-image=registry.access.redhat.com/ubi9/openjdk-21-runtime:1.24` (or `openjdk-17-runtime:1.24`)."
msgstr ""

msgid "With OpenShift S2I builds: `quarkus.openshift.base-jvm-image=registry.access.redhat.com/ubi9/openjdk-21:1.24` (or `openjdk-17:1.24`)."
msgstr ""

msgid "Workaround, if you cannot rebuild yet"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If you cannot rebuild and redeploy your images right away, add `-XX:-UseBASE64Intrinsics` to `JAVA_OPTS_APPEND`.\n"
"It can be set at deployment time, without rebuilding the image.\n"
"Use `JAVA_OPTS_APPEND`, not `JAVA_OPTS`: overriding `JAVA_OPTS` drops the defaults computed by the image."
msgstr ""

msgid "In your `Dockerfile.jvm`:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Or at deployment time, without rebuilding the image (Kubernetes / OpenShift):"
msgstr ""

msgid "If you use the Quarkus Kubernetes extension:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Native executable compiled with Mandrel"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Not affected.\n"
"A native executable does not run on the HotSpot JVM and does not use the vulnerable intrinsic.\n"
"No action needed."
msgstr ""

msgid "Builder images (Mandrel and GraalVM CE)"
msgstr ""

msgid "These images let you build a native executable without installing Mandrel or GraalVM (`quarkus.native.container-build=true`)."
msgstr ""

msgid "The resulting native executables are *not affected*, whatever image version built them."
msgstr ""

msgid "Why patch the builder images?"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The `native-image` tool is a Java application.\n"
"It runs on the JDK shipped in the builder image.\n"
"That JDK is affected.\n"
"The build only processes your own code and dependencies, so the risk is low.\n"
"Still, we do not want to ship images with an unprotected vulnerable JDK.\n"
"So we applied the workaround to the builder images."
msgstr ""

